-artificial-intelligence-bias">What is Artificial Intelligence Bias</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/who-is-the-father-of-artificial-intelligence">Who is the Father of Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/sop-for-ms-in-artificial-intelligence">SOP for MS in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/top-ai-penny-stocks-in-india">Top AI Penny Stocks in India</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-ai-in-data-analytics">What is AI in Data Analytics</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-business-management">Artificial Intelligence in Business Management</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/partial-order-planning-in-artificial-intelligence">Partial Order Planning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-auditing">Artificial Intelligence in Auditing</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-construction">Artificial Intelligence in Construction</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/information-retrieval-in-artificial-intelligence">Information Retrieval in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-psychology">Artificial Intelligence in Psychology</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/iterative-deepening-search-in-artificial-intelligence">Iterative Deepening Search in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/applications-of-deep-learning-in-artificial-intelligence">Applications of Deep Learning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/7-artificial-intelligence-projects-in-agriculture">7 Artificial Intelligence Projects in Agriculture</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-multimodal-ai">What is Multimodal AI</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/benefits-of-artificial-intelligence-in-business">Benefits of Artificial Intelligence in Business</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conceptual-graphs-in-artificial-intelligence">Conceptual Graphs in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/problem-space-and-search-in-artificial-intelligence">Problem Space and Search in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conditional-planning-in-artificial-intelligence">Conditional Planning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/informed-search-algorithms-in-artificial-intelligence">Informed Search Algorithms in Artificial Intelligence</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading10"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse10" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse10" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Related Tutorials</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading10" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse10"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tensorflow">Tensorflow Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch">PyTorch Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reinforcement-learning">Reinforcement Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="ai-upscaling">next →</a> <a class="next" href="problem-characteristics-in-ai">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">Artificial Intelligence in Judiciary</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>14 Apr 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 12 min read</span></div><p>The Supreme Court of India is on the front line in embracing the use of AI to improve all the different dimensions of its activities. It has one of the most significant applications: in judicial document translation, legal information becomes more accessible to the audience. Since February 2023, the court has been intensively utilizing AI for the transcription of oral arguments, mainly in the important constitution bench tasks. This new practice not only makes it more efficient but also authenticates and timely documentation of procedures. The Supreme Court of India also formed a committee to make justice accessible to the public, as well as to promote legal literacy, and a distinguished judge heads this. The function of this committee is to supervise the important task of translating Supreme Court decisions and High Court judgments into various vernacular languages. The AI translation committees are set up in several high counts, diligently overseeing the comprehensive process of translating supreme court and high court judgments into regional languages. The goal of this essential initiative is to increase accessibility to legal information for a large audience, especially those who may not be good at English. To date, eight High Courts have successfully implemented the electronic <a href="high-court">High Court</a> Reports, which make translated judgments accessible through the digital medium. The rest of the High Courts are in the process of rolling out their e-HCRs, thereby further reaching the masses with legal information through transparency in the judicial process.</p><p>AI has dramatically and rapidly transformed the judicial map around the globe. From law enforcement agencies to courts and prosecution services, AI is increasingly adopted to streamline processes and increase investigative capabilities. In criminal justice, for example, <a href="artificial-intelligence-ai">AI</a> systems are used to assist the investigation, analyze large amounts of data, and even automate certain decision-making tasks.</p><p>To mitigate these concerns and promote responsible adoption of AI, a collaborative effort is underway to develop a Massive Online Open Course (MOOC) in partnership with UNESCO's Category 2 Centre CETIC and the IEEE. This venture aims to equip judges, lawyers, and stakeholders with the necessary information and instruments to facilitate the understanding of complexities associated with the use of AI in courts. This MOOC ensures that all benefits that derive from AI are coupled with safety for human rights and integrity of justice principles.</p><h2 class="h2">Judicial Actor's Role in AI Governance:</h2><p>AI technologies are developing and present a challenging legal landscape, and the judicial systems are dealing with novel questions surrounding human rights, surveillance, and liability. Conversely, AI holds the potential to simplify procedures and improve efficiency while discussing the transparency, neutrality, and human oversight of automated decision-making. As AI algorithms become more sophisticated, it is important to confirm that they are developed and deployed in a way that aligns with the principles of human rights because of the process and the law rule.</p><p>The combination of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the criminal justice system is transforming at a rapid rate how judicial processes are conducted worldwide. Several judicial systems, containing courts, prosecution services, and specialized legal books, are actively exploring the potential of AI to increase efficiency and accuracy. AI methods are being used to assist in criminal investigations, analyze large amounts of case-related data, and automate routine administrative methods that allow human professionals to focus on more aspects that are complete of justice delivery. In addition, AI-driven tools are coming to be developed to enable decision-making processes that provide data-driven insights to judges and prosecution while validating transparency and consistency. With this technological advancement, one enjoys workflow plus the promise of reducing human biases, which improves outcomes in cases and addresses challenges at the systemic level in criminal justice.</p><p>AI is revolutionizing the criminal justice system by introducing innovative solutions by enhancing efficiency, accuracy, and fairness.</p><h2 class="h2">Initiative of UNESCO Judges:</h2><p>Since 2014, <a href="unesco-united-nations-educational-scientific-and-cultural-organization">UNESCO</a>, in association with its partners, has carried on its efforts of sensitization in active training for judges and professionals judicially to major matters that include freedom of speech expression, public access to information and safety of journalists in an effort conducted more particularly via MOOCs - the massive open online course allowing broad participation. To date, more than 17,000 judicial actors have benefited from these training programs, with a strong focus on regions such as Latin America and Africa. In Latin America, UNESCO has worked closely with the Inter-American Court of Human Rights to deliver tailored training initiatives. In Africa, UNESCO has collaborated with several key institutions: the Centre for Human Rights at the University of Pretoria, the African Commission on Human and Peoples' Rights, the African Court on Human and Peoples' Rights, and the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS). These collaborations will enhance knowledge and application of international standards to freedom of expression and freedom of the press, helping journalists work in safe environments and ensure public access to information. In doing this, UNESCO contributes to more general efforts to strengthen the democratic principle and human rights worldwide.</p><p>Training through these courses increases judicial actors' knowledge about international and regional law-making frameworks aimed at protecting and preserving such basic rights. The courses also deal with issues of the digital era. Courses dealing with established legal standards consider and discuss emerging issues in the face of this new technology age that the Internet and other forms of digital technology are producing and that create complex issues with respect to the issues of expression online, privacy, misinformation, and journalist safety in these digital arenas. Such programs would ultimately be aimed toward fostering a judiciary that will effectively work for the protection of the rights of both individuals and media persons so that free expression is safe both in traditional and digital mediums.</p><p>Judicial actors who are familiar with the subtleties of international conventions, the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, for example, and regional human rights instruments act in such a way as to ensure that their decision-making is in line with principles of free speech and press freedom while addressing journalists' challenges. This understanding also contributes to a uniform and coherent approach toward the interpretation of laws, whereby judges can make informed, fair, and just decisions that respect not only national legal systems but also international and regional human rights standards. The entire legal system will reap the fruits in the long run through education and training of the judiciary to be responsive and knowledgeable to serve justice in a manner that upholds the rule of law so that journalists' rights are protected. Thus, freedom of expression flourishes, and media workers work safely without fear.</p><p>Building on the success of their early initiatives, UNESCO and its partners are now extending efforts into a new program: enhancing the capacity of judicial actors to approach the increasing presence of artificial intelligence in courts and law enforcement. The new training is to arm judges and other members of the judicial profession with all the knowledge and skills enabling them to understand and efficiently use AI technologies in any legal context. The program has been focused on the complex implications that AI brings into legal decision-making processes so as to ensure that such decisions take place in accordance with international expectations for human rights. A vital need for the judiciary is to understand how AI tools work and their potential biases because AI-based systems are increasingly being implemented into legal processes. Capacity building in this initiative shall bolster the understanding of the challenges related to AI within the ethical, legal, and human rights dimensions to make judicial actors better choices while respecting human rights and embracing technological developments. The program will focus on the areas of AI and human rights to strengthen the judiciary and uphold justice in an age where technology is rapidly rewriting the rules of legal practices and law enforcement methods.</p><h2 class="h2">Comparative Analysis of the Use of AI in the Judiciary:</h2><h3 class="h3">USA:</h3><p>The COMPAS tool, for example, is a tool powered by AI that is used to change the judicial process by helping judges assess the risk of offenders. The system considers many factors, such as criminal history, socioeconomic background, and mental health, to predict the likelihood of an individual reoffending. Such tools allow courts to make more informed decisions, balancing public safety with rehabilitation opportunities. Similarly, the United States Sentencing Commission uses AI to create and implement guidelines for sentencing with uniformity, fairness, and proportionality in terms of punishment. In addition to risk assessment, the US judicial system uses AI to perform a lot of day-to-day work that has been optimized to make the judicial process more efficient and accessible. Chatbots powered by AI answer basic questions about court procedures, schedules, and related matters. The workloads of the court staff are much lessened due to the work done by virtual assistants, so these professionals are busy only doing the tough things. For the layperson, access to this accurate information makes all legal procedures quite accessible. In effect, through this use of AI, the judiciary reduces bottlenecks in their functioning and further facilitates accessibility of justice to every sector of the public.</p><h3 class="h3">China:</h3><p>Smart Courts are what China calls a very intelligent system that combines state-of-the-art AI techniques with traditional judicial practices and judgment-making. It allows the court to analyze past judgments along with applicable law as well as judicial precedents of different cases and presents some information from it for the judges so that their judgments are accurate as well as consistent with similar precedents. This approach not only improves the productivity of the judiciary but also delivers justice on time. Apart from judgment, Chinese courts are utilizing AI for more inclusive legal research. Amongst them is the 'China Judgements Online' platform, which applies AI to make access to court documents easier. The judiciary, as well as other members of the legal fraternity, can access relevant judgments, statutes and case analysis more promptly, thereby cutting research time. This platform brings about the increased transparency of the judiciary and consistency in judgments. Its incorporation of AI in not only decision-making but also in research is going to form a benchmark for modernizing various court systems across the world for the Chinese judiciary.</p><h3 class="h3">United Kingdom:</h3><p>In the first quarter of 2020, the UK Ministry of Justice launched its pioneering Digital Case System, which was meant to transform crown court functioning. It will ensure that current case hearing updates are received by both the legal fraternity and the staff in court at the same time. The Bar Council's Ethics Committee has issued guidelines on specific matters relating to the access of criminal law barristers to the online portal and the usage of the system. The guidelines cover issues such as data security and confidentiality and how digital evidence should be handled in court. These will have the effect of being more efficient, accessible, and environmentally conscious in judicial processes.</p><h2 class="h2">Legal Framework for Regulating AI: Global and Indian Perspectives</h2><p>Transformational opportunities in healthcare, education, transportation, and entertainment- that's artificial intelligence for you. A double-edged sword with massive benefits and risks in the context of dilemmas on ethics, privacy breaches, biases from algorithms, discrimination, and exposure to cyber vulnerabilities. These needs require solutions so that the safe and fair development of AI benefits happens for all. To address these issues, a global AI experts and data scientists consortium has introduced a holistic voluntary framework in the promotion of the responsible development and deployment of AI technologies. Spearheaded by the World Ethical Data Foundation (WEDF), which has more than 25,000 members and contributors from leading technology companies such as Meta, Google, and Samsung. A framework shall thereby be provided for developers to consider the ethical and social implications of their AI projects, with in-depth sets of detailed questions ranging from 84 that they are encouraged to discuss in the early phases of AI system design and implementation. Questions they put forward relate to the really pertinent areas of AI development, including openness and the mitigation of bias. Besides, protection of user's information and evaluation of potential risks faced by society. The mechanism fosters introspection in tandem with proactive planning to formulate a culture of accountability with reliance on AI innovation. This is an essential step in an inclusive technological future at the time of the world's search for a balance between the luring benefits of AI vs. the risks it poses.</p><h3 class="h3">Indian Perspective:</h3><p>There are no specific laws in India at present that govern Artificial Intelligence. The primary executive agency that deals with strategies regarding AI is the Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology. MEITY has been proactive by constituting various committees to develop a comprehensive policy framework for AI governance and regulation. To guide ethical AI development and deployment, India's premier policy think tank, <a href="niti-aayog-full-form">NITI Aayog</a>, has formulated a set of seven principles for responsible AI. Representing safety and dependability, security, transparency, accountability, and the protection of positive human values, these guiding principles are designed to ensure that the design and use of AI technology benefit society while safeguarding individual rights. The Supreme Court of India and the high courts, on constitutional grounds, have the mandate to enforce fundamental rights, including the right to privacy, which was recently declared a fundamental right in the landmark case of Justice K.S. Puttaswamy vs Union of India. This judgment sets the basis for addressing privacy issues regarding AI technologies.</p><p>India's primary legislation that deals with data protection is the Information Technology Act of 2000, along with the rules that go with it. However, feeling the need to have stronger data protection laws, the government has presented the Digital Personal Data Protection Bill, which MEITY heads. Even though this bill is not yet passed into law, it is a step forward in how personal data will be collected, processed, and stored. Once the bill is passed, it will allow the individual the right to question what is being collected on him and by whom, both by the private and government sectors and how that information is being processed and stored.</p><h2 class="h2">Challenges and Risks in The Legal Sector:</h2><h3 class="h3">Data Privacy and Confidentiality:</h3><p>AI systems usually rely on tremendous volumes of data in their training process, learning patterns, and prediction. Hence, organizations applying AI need to operate within some demanding legal frameworks so that such data can be collected and stored for processes made in accordance with established law. For example, mishandled sensitive data is likely to violate privacy as well as cause regulatory infractions, as information misuse poses a significant risk to both the organizational reputation and the legal account. There is this constant tension between the necessity to constantly have large datasets available for refining AI accuracy against the legal and ethical mandate to maintain the privacy of individuals. As data protection laws are changing all over the world, the jurisdictional challenge is that organizations need to address compliance with diverse regulations in different regions. This is most applicable when data is being transferred across borders or used in a global AI model. This shows that there is an urgent need for governance mechanisms that are strong and transparent data practices, as well as ethical AI frameworks that reduce risks and promote the development of lawful, responsible AI.</p><h3 class="h3">Issues Related to Licensing and Accountability:</h3><p>Just recently, an incident showed the risks of uncontrolled dependency on generative AI in legal practice. A New York lawyer used <a href="what-is-chatgpt">ChatGPT</a> for legal research and cited six cases in a brief submitted to the court, but they could not be found by the opposing counsel, leading to an investigation. Lawyers must, therefore, practice due diligence and verify AI-generated content to avoid professional misconduct, reputational harm or even prosecution. With AI evolving continuously, both legal professionals and regulatory bodies need to set up clear guidelines or best practices for the responsible usage of AI in legal contexts. Such mechanisms include defining accountability, transparency of recommendations made through AI systems, and the general ethical basis for the protection of the integrity of the profession. Unlike licensed attorneys, AI systems do not need to have licensure in order to practice law. That is to say, the AI system is not under the same ethical standards or professional codes of conduct as a human lawyer. This raises one critical accountability issue: Who is liable if faulty, misleading, or even harmful legal advice arises from an AI system? Should the blame lie with the developer who created the system or the user who relied upon the output of the system without any verification?</p><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="ai-upscaling">AI Upscaling</a></span></div><br/><br/><div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="problem-characteristics-in-ai" style="float:left">← prev</a> <a class="next" href="ai-upscaling" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br/><br/></td></tr></tbody></table></div></body></html><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="how-blockchain-and-ai-complement-each-other"><h3 class="h3">How Blockchain and AI Complement each other?</h3><p class="related-post-content">Introduction Blockchain and AI can be considered the two most significant and arising technologies today. While AI has exceeded all its limits of experimenting with performances in the labs, blockchain is versatile and easy to use. Both though are, however, rooted in different areas, but they have...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 8 min read <span class="arrow-position"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1.3rem" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 512 512" width="1.3rem" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M295.6 163.7c-5.1 5-5.1 13.3-.1 18.4l60.8 60.9H124.9c-7.1 0-12.9 5.8-12.9 13s5.8 13 12.9 13h231.3l-60.8 60.9c-5 5.1-4.9 13.3.1 18.4 5.1 5 13.2 5 18.3-.1l82.4-83c1.1-1.2 2-2.5 2.7-4.1.7-1.6 1-3.3 1-5 0-3.4-1.3-6.6-3.7-9.1l-82.4-83c-4.9-5.2-13.1-5.3-18.2-.3z"></path></svg></span></p></a></li><li><a class="related-post-item" href="semantics-of-propositional-logic-in-artific